  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The cost of waiting for full price</h2>
    <p>Holding an item until the "right" buyer appears looks free, but it isn't. Every month an item sits, you pay in 3 ways: storage space or fees, the cash tied up in it that could be buying faster-moving stock, and the risk that it goes out of season, out of style, or down in value.</p>
    <p>A simple example: an item could sell for $100 in about 6 months, or you have an $80 offer today. If storage costs $2 a month and your cash could otherwise earn 10% a year, the $100 sale is worth about $95.35 in today's money, less $12 of storage: roughly $83. The $80 offer is close, and if the 6-month sale is uncertain, it's probably the better choice. The <a href="../../calculators/inventory-markdown.html">inventory markdown calculator</a> runs this comparison with your own numbers and adds a liquidation option.</p>

    <h2>Signs it's time to mark down</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Views without sales.</strong> An item that gets attention but no offers is usually priced above what buyers will pay.</li>
      <li><strong>Seasonality.</strong> Coats in March and swimwear in September lose their best buyers for months. Holding them through the off-season adds storage for a sale that's still uncertain.</li>
      <li><strong>Your own turnover.</strong> If most of your inventory sells within 30 to 60 days, an item at 120 days is an outlier taking up space that faster sellers could use.</li>
      <li><strong>Falling comparables.</strong> When recent sold prices for the same item are dropping, waiting tends to cost more, not less.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Think in turnover, not just margin</h2>
    <p>A lower margin that sells quickly can beat a higher margin that sells slowly. $15 profit on an item that sells in 2 weeks lets you reinvest that cash about 26 times a year; $30 profit on one that takes 6 months turns over only twice. For a reseller with limited cash and space, how quickly money comes back usually matters as much as the profit on any single sale.</p>
  </div>
</section>
